{#
  Confirmation for a service-contributed deployment action (see DeploymentAction).

  Loaded into the shared modal shell by openServiceModal(), so a service's action asks
  the same way as everything else on this page instead of through window.confirm().
  The endpoint is filled in server-side from the action the service offered; this
  template never sees a URL from the request.

  Expects: action (DeploymentAction), message.

  The CSRF header and the swap live on the wrapping div, NOT on the button: htmx
  inherits both from an ancestor, and a ROOS <c-button> re-emits attribute values in
  double quotes, which mangles the JSON of hx-headers. The action endpoints answer
  JSON, hence hx-swap="none" -- project-details.html.j2 reloads the page on success and
  fills #deployment-action-error on failure.

  These endpoints are NOT quick. Sleeping a deployment commits to git and then runs the
  whole reprocessing pipeline, ArgoCD sync included, while the request stays open. So the
  waiting state is not decoration: without it the dialog sits there unchanged for tens of
  seconds and the only reasonable conclusion is that the button did nothing. Driven by
  hx-indicator, so every service action gets it without asking for it.
#}
<div data-deployment-action="true"
     hx-headers='{"X-CSRF-Token": "{{ request.state.csrf_token }}"}'
     hx-swap="none"
     hx-indicator="#deployment-action-busy, #deployment-action-buttons">
    <div class="edit-section-content">
        <c-layout-flow gap="md">
            <c-p>{{ message }}</c-p>
            <div id="deployment-action-error" class="edit-section-error" style="display: none;"></div>
            {# htmx puts .htmx-request on the indicator targets for the duration of the
               request; the stylesheet turns that into "show this, hide the buttons".

               The id sits on this wrapper and NOT on the c-alert: ROOS copies an id onto
               an inner element too, so the document would carry it twice and the
               hx-indicator selector would be ambiguous. The wrapper is the behaviour
               hook, the alert is the component that draws it. #}
            <div id="deployment-action-busy" class="deployment-action-busy">
                <c-alert kind="info" content="{{ busy_message }}" />
            </div>
        </c-layout-flow>
    </div>
    <div id="deployment-action-buttons" class="edit-section-actions">
        <c-button type="button" label="Annuleren" kind="tertiary" @click="closeEditModal()" />
        <c-button
            type="button"
            label="{{ action.label }}"
            kind="{{ action.kind }}"
            showIcon="before"
            icon="{{ action.icon }}"
            hx-post="{{ action.endpoint }}"
            hx-disabled-elt="this" />
    </div>
</div>
